Well, yes and no. On the one hand, you are putting a permanent weapon
of mass-monster-instadeath into the hands of the PC. On the other, you
are putting a permanent weapon of mass-player-instadeath into the
hands of the RNG. A player wielding a permatrice corpse would be sooo
tempted to wander about with it, which of course is just begging to
die by foocubus-degloving/ pitfalling/ wrong-inventory-letter-eating
and so on. It's these little temptations to YASD that make the RNG
seem so devious, and the game so challenging.

That said, I will agree with nyra and say that the permatrice corpse
should be nerfed somehow. Luckily, I have a suggestion as to exactly
how: Fragility. Here's an experiment you can try at home.

1- Go to the butchers and buy a whole chicken, preferably one with
head and feathers still. Otherwise, a roadkill pheasant or something
will probably do just as well.
2- Don a pair of gloves. Gauntlets, if you have them.
3- Wield your dead bird by the leg or neck.
4- Violently and repeatedly thwack said fowl against a large, hostile
animal. (If you can't find a hostile animal, a peaceful one will soon
become hostile once you start with the thwacking.)
5- See how long it takes before bits start falling off your bird.

Implemented in nethack, you'd end up with something like
g - an uncursed incorruptible somewhat battered cockatrice corpse.

The idea is, after a certain amount of abuse the corpse would fall to
pieces completely. A happy side-effect is that you could use it to
easily re-balance the average SPC [1] if you happen to think it needs
re-balancing at all. A perversely nethackish (or nethackishly
perverse, both are good) twist would be link corpse-deterioration to
the wielder's strength, so that a weak PC/NPC would get a greater SPC
than a strong one. Naturally throwing corpses, kicking them, dropping
them down stairs and so on would damage them too. Also, it wouldn't
just be 'trice corpses subject to damage. Any corpse (unless
convincing arguments can be made for 'indestructible' corpses) would
be subject to the same effects.

> In general, rotproofing a corpse for food reasons would practically
> never be worth a scroll:

I agree. This technique would be used almost exclusively for lizards,
trices, and certain other lightweight corpses that you might not have
the stomach/ poison resistance to eat just now: floating eyes, killer
bees and so on. I don't see this as a problem though. There are plenty
of features of nethack that are used only rarely, and I think they add
to the game rather than detract from it.

> corpses have very bad nutrition-to-weight
> ratios, and if you want the corpse for intrinsics, the requirement for
> a tinning kit or enough room in your stomach are deliberate design
> decisions; allowing to bypass them would on the whole damage, not
> improve gameplay.

But you wouldn't be bypassing those methods cost-free:

1 - Remember you'd need both a non-cursed scroll of FD and a safe
source of confusion (if there is such a thing) to proof a corpse. A
player is unlikely to have both of these available at just the right
moment in the early game, when most of the intrinsic-gathering takes
place. By the time you've got a unihorn and forgotten spell, you
should have at least poison and sleep resistance already, probably
telepathy, and quite likely heat or cold resistance, or both. And all
of them are available through crowning when you hit your first
friendly altar anyway. Therefore the only edible intrinsic you're
likely to be desperately hunting for is teleport control, so this
isn't going to be taking too much business away from eat-on-the-spot.

2 - This method does not remove negative properties of the corpse (ie
poison) like tinning does.

3 - You need to pick up the corpse to 'proof it. Many useful corpses
(dragons) are too heavy to lift anyway, so it certainly doesn't make
the tinning kit obselete. Also, a t-kit has multiple charges, but by
the time you have more than a handful of ?oFD you probably don't need
them.

4 - Larder management. Unlike eating from the floor or tinning, this
technique leaves the corpse at its full weight- you can leave it on
the floor if you want, but be sure to keep Tiddles away from it, and
beware of passing g-cubes. ideally, you'd either carry a 'proofed
corpse around with you until you could eat it, or lug it to your
stash. No problem for lizards and newts, but larger monsters could
cause difficulties.
Remember what I said earlier about tempation to YASD? I consider of a
rotproofed mindflayer corpse as a YASD-by-encumberance trap that
balances any potential exploits quite nicely. This might also
introduce some interesting new tactics, luring heavy monsters into pet-
proof rooms for the slaughter and so on.

5 - The cost of the scroll itself. I can't remember the last time I
actually read a ?oFD. Blanking them all the moment you ID them is a no-
brainer, and no-brainers bore me. If ?oFD actually had some value,
because you want to save one for your first lizard corpse, then that
would be a Good Thing imho.


